+## 198.2 - (Fixed/Improved) Base AdvCiv AI non-combat food-production allocation could stall small growing cities instead of using mature or stagnant food/production pumps (observed with Workers)
+
+Follow-up to KI#198. The underlying allocation behavior is inherited from Base AdvCiv: after a city decided that a food-production civilian was wanted, there was no central cross-city check asking whether that city was a sensible place to freeze growth while training it. AdvCiv-SAS's stronger Worker floor made this long-standing inefficiency much easier to observe because Workers were requested more reliably. A young population-1/2 city could therefore spend 10-40+ turns on a Worker even when a mature city on the same landmass could convert much more food plus production into the same unit in only a few turns. This did not reduce the requested Worker count; it made the empire pay for that Worker with unnecessarily expensive lost growth and delayed use. Workers provide the empirical reproduction, but the optimization belongs at the generic non-combat food-production layer rather than in Worker-specific production branches.
+
+There had been related older work, which explains why this issue could feel familiar without actually having been solved generically. AdvCiv-SAS had already restricted Settler production to the capital/high population specifically to exploit food-is-production efficiently and avoid small cities spending many turns stalled on Settlers. That older Settler rule addresses the same economic principle through a specialized restriction. Conversely, the later KI#198 Worker-floor change deliberately did not restrict Workers to only high-population/capital cities because weaker or stagnant cities can sometimes contribute efficiently and because broad restrictions could slow emergency replacement. What was still missing was a central cross-city comparison after a fresh non-combat food-production choice had already passed its ordinary need/priority/odds logic.
+
+The new allocation rule is therefore a gate rather than a fixed-city restriction. For a fresh non-combat food-production unit, any safe city that can finish within `SAS_AI_CHOOSE_UNIT_NONCOMBAT_FOOD_PRODUCTION_MAX_TURNS` (5 Normal-speed-equivalent turns by default, scaled by game-speed `TrainPercent`) is already considered good enough and keeps the choice immediately. This intentionally allows multiple cities to rebuild Workers in parallel after heavy losses. Existing invested production is also always completed. Only a slower fresh choice looks for another safe city on the same landmass that can train the unit at least as quickly; if no such city exists, the current/best available producer still builds instead of waiting.
+
+Among eligible alternatives, population and stagnation choose the preferred food/hammer pump rather than a fragile turns-saved formula. The largest eligible population is the default preference, because larger cities can usually convert much more food and hammer output while the unit freezes growth. However, a currently stagnant city is preferred when it is within `SAS_AI_CHOOSE_UNIT_NONCOMBAT_FOOD_PRODUCTION_STAGNANT_POPULATION_GAP` of that largest city (3 by default, using a strict comparison: population 8 can beat population 10, population 7 cannot). This makes otherwise-unused food productive and deliberately allows a strong stagnant pump to build several Workers in sequence. Actual production turns only provide the fast-enough/sanity gate and equal-population tie-break; they are not the strategic score.
+
+The implementation is generic for non-combat `isFoodProduction()` units and excludes combat-capable, air-combat and nuclear units so a future modmod that creates a food-production fighter is not unnecessarily constrained. Workers are the important currently affected unit in AdvCiv-SAS. Settlers are conceptually part of the same food-production allocation problem, but `UNITAI_SETTLE` is currently exempt because AdvCiv-SAS separately allows Settlers only from the capital; without the exemption, the capital could defer a Settler to a non-capital that current production policy would never actually request to build it. The existing capital-only Settler rule therefore remains the specialized Settler solution for now. If that restriction is later removed, this generic allocator can take over Settler allocation by removing that one role exemption.
+
+The full `BBAI_20260809T054807Z_load1.log` / `SASGameRecord_20260809T054807Z_load1.log` validation ran through turn 391. The new allocator produced 87 actual `DEFER_TO_PREFERRED_CITY` decisions. Their median source city was population 2 with a 9-turn Worker, while the median preferred city was population 8 with a 3-turn Worker. Representative cases included Memphis population 1 / 12 turns -> Thebes population 5 / 4 turns, New York population 1 / 15 -> Washington population 5 / 3, Nippur population 1 / 15 -> Babylon population 8 / 4, and Huamanga population 1 / 45 -> Cuzco population 11 / 3. Of the 87 redirects, 48 selected a stagnant preferred city, showing that the stagnation preference materially affects allocation rather than merely documenting intent.
+
+The opposite safety behavior was also directly visible. There were 63 KEEP decisions from population-4-or-smaller cities that could complete the Worker within the 5-turn Normal-speed gate, confirming that efficient side cities still participate in rapid replacement. The allocator also did not create a waiting deadlock: of 85 deferrals with enough later log history to observe another allocation event before game end, all 85 saw that empire reach a later KEEP Worker decision within 5 turns and 80 did so within 2 turns. Worker availability remained high in the GameRecord as well; for example, the USA had 11 Workers for 6 cities at turn 100 with 9 actively building improvements, while India later had 24 Workers at turn 300. The change therefore improves which city pays the food-production growth cost rather than lowering the KI#198 Worker force.
+
+`FOOD_PRODUCTION_ALLOCATION` remains available through the dedicated Worker BBAI log: level 2 records the unusual deferrals, while level 3 can additionally record ordinary KEEP decisions. The turn-391 diagnostic run temporarily used Worker log level 3 and produced a very large BBAI file mostly because the existing full Worker scan diagnostics are intentionally exhaustive; the committed/default Worker log level is restored to 0.
+
+Fixed/improved with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.
