@@ -228,6 +228,7 @@ Note: cultural victory bullets as of now assume 3 legendary cities are required,
 - **Optional first economic unit before the capital's first defender:** when the first and only capital is not in danger, it can produce one immediately useful Work Boat or required land Worker before filling an empty city garrison. This should be more efficient because it avoids delaying the first improvement and the growth, production, and bonuses it unlocks, and animals are not a serious city-capture threat. However, because a human can try an unusually early rush against the temporarily empty capital, this tradeoff is XML-tunable and can be disabled to restore Base AdvCiv's defender-first behavior. This is as of now only applicable to the first city, so the unchanged no-defender priority resumes after this first economic unit.
 - **Prioritize useful Work Boats for seafood before land Workers:** after producing an immediate defender when needed, a safe AI city now builds a Work Boat before its land Worker when it has seafood that can be improved now (unless immediate defense and danger/Turtle safeguards take precedence). This is more efficient because improving seafood increases food without stopping city growth during production, and this extra food helps the following Worker and later production be produced faster. Note: the AI does not waste a Work Boat on unavailable improvements such as Whale before the required technology like as of now `TECH_SEAFARING` is discovered. Note: also remove the Base AdvCiv population-3 cutoff that stopped Work Boat production midway (See [KI#174](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#174---fixedimproved-base-advciv-work-boat-icitypopulation--3-cutoff-in-cvcityaiai_chooseproduction-interrupted-seafood-production-for-a-land-worker)). After these changes, in save file 443 BBAI tests, Seoul successfully selected a Work Boat for its available Molluscs on turn 5 instead of delaying it until about turn 17 (to produce a worker before it), while Mutal correctly built its Worker first because Maya lacked the as of now required `TECH_FISHING` for Molluscs and Fish, then selected a Work Boat on turn 12 when this tech was discovered and thus these bonuses became improvable.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6060+) **Isolated cities no longer produce land civilian units that cannot perform their role:** for example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed the one-tile island city Bactra producing a Worker that remained idle because none of its plots could be improved; the same could happen, for example, on a landmass containing only plots the AI cannot improve. To fix this, the Worker-demand calculation no longer creates a minimum need when no local plot can be improved, and the AI now also refuses a Worker when its landmass already has enough, as well as land Explorer units when no exploration remains there, and land Spy units in a secondary isolated colony with neither a known rival city nor an adjacent Spy transport. Primary landmasses remain eligible so an overseas empire can still produce Spies and their transports. Note: this check is central in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` to make sure no short-circuit or unexpected logic bypasses it, as with AdvCiv-SAS's central unit-scrapping safeguards. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6095+) **Produce non-combat food-production units in suitable cities instead of stalling small/remote city growth:** small or newly founded cities could spend a very long time producing units for which food counts as production (`<bFood>` / `bFoodIsProduction`) (in AdvCiv-SAS, especially Workers), stopping their population growth even when a larger city could supply the same unit very quickly from its much higher total food yield (due to high population) and generally higher hammer yield. This was particularly wasteful when a mature city was already stagnant, because using it as the producer costs little or no growth there. In save file 460 BBAI testing, examples included population-1 Huamanga needing 45 turns for a Worker while population-11 Cuzco could finish one in 3 turns; across the full turn-391 validation run, 87 slow Worker choices were redirected, with the median source at population 2 / 9 turns and the median preferred producer at population 8 / 3 turns. The AI now keeps any fresh choice that is already fast enough (5 Normal-speed-equivalent turns by default, scaled by game speed), so several capable cities can still produce Workers in parallel after heavy losses; otherwise it looks for a safe same-area city that can produce the unit at least as fast, preferring a stagnant city when it is within 3 population of the largest eligible producer and otherwise preferring the largest population. If all cities are slow, the best available producer still builds it, and existing invested production is never abandoned. This logic is generic for non-combat food-production units rather than Worker-specific; Settlers have the same underlying production/growth tradeoff but are currently handled by AdvCiv-SAS's existing capital-only Settler rule and therefore remain exempt from this allocator. The same validation run also had 63 population-4-or-smaller cities correctly keep Workers they could complete within the fast-production gate. See [KI#198.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1982---fixedimproved-base-advciv-ai-non-combat-food-production-allocation-could-stall-small-growing-cities-instead-of-using-mature-or-stagnant-foodproduction-pumps-observed-with-workers).
 - **Emergency water-food / water-hammer buildings (pre-fallback):** Before the "no-production" fallback runs, if a **coastal (ocean-connected)** city is **low food per turn**, force a **water-food building class** (e.g., **Harbor** or civ variants that give +1 food on water tiles) to jump-start growth. Also force the **water-hammer building class** (e.g., **Port**) for **high-enough pop** (enough headcount so it is worth investing on water plots) coastal cities unless they are very good and don't need it: cities that **still have fairly low production** or **not too high food surplus**; Port can take significant time, but these cities are **unlikely to contribute much short-term production anyway**, and hammer on water tiles makes their later infrastructure and future building time **more helpful later on**. Immediate danger / defense logic still runs earlier, so Walls/Castle can still go first when needed, otherwise the **risk is deemed worth the delay** to produce these buildings. Runs in `CvCity::doTurn`.
 - **Emergency defense priority (risk-aware):** When invasion risk is high—enemy team **significantly stronger**, **nearby danger** detected, or **at war** with an opponent not significantly weaker—**force-build** **Walls**, then **Castle** (if missing) at top priority. Triggers are conservative to avoid growth drag. Implemented in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. Early autoplay suggests fewer snowball steamrolls. **Update:** Skip this emergency defense path for **very low-hammer** cities (scaled by era) or cities whose inner BFC is **mostly water tiles**, as these are better used as economy cities and unlikely to hold the main front; this also prevents triggering fallback-unit / emergency-defense builds too early and avoids stalling early growth. **Update 2:** For **water-heavy cities**, this emergency-defense path is **disabled**; see the related Update 2 note below.
 - **"No production" stall — worked around / fixed**: AI cities no longer sit on **no production**. Added a robust fallback: produce the **most expensive suitable land combat unit**; if a candidate is **extremely overpriced for the era**, switch to the **cheapest suitable** instead. **Civilians** (e.g., scouts) are excluded. This prevents idle turns (e.g., will build an archer in the Ancient era) and cuts wasted hammers. Added in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. See [KI#51](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#51---worked-around--fixed-massive-seemingly-base-advciv---civ4-issue-if-im-not-mistaken-of-many-cities-entering-no-production-early-for-1-or-several-turns-many-times-during-the-game-early-and-possibly-later-this-is-why-many-cities-have-a-process-rather-than-no-production-as-processes-are-not-available-early-and-are-listed-among-fallbacks-if-production-fails-it-seems-but-check-to-be-sure). Related: handicap settings were **slightly reduced** to keep difficulty even with the new efficiency. **Update:** Added **offense-only** and **defense-only** selection modes inside this fallback (auto-reverting to "any suitable land unit" if no candidates exist). This is intended to avoid bypassing `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` limits and to reduce excessive **Longbowmen** when the fallback triggers. **Update 2:** For **water-heavy cities**, when the **no-production fallback** in `CvCity::AI_doTurn` triggers, try to use key **water-focused economic buildings** (food / hammers / gold) as the fallback instead of a generic land-unit fallback; this is usually more efficient for growth/economy, and such cities are less likely to be main land-attack targets. See also [Buildings](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md#buildings) or Sevopedia/XML.

